# command: real-probe.py, once per run below, each a throwaway interactive Claude Code session (Haiku) through a pseudo-terminal; then timeline.py and real-collect.py on what hookwrap.sh kept
# date: 2026-11-13, 22:49 to 23:09 UTC
# by: Fable 5.2 (reviewer), on the operator's Mac (darwin arm64), Claude Code 2.1.325, with handoff built at 345a0e704ad502e54fbf0d4c947bc296486975cb
#
# The session ran in a scratch git repository with no .handoff/ of its own, so the operator's installed
# hooks did nothing there. Its .claude/settings.local.json ran hookwrap.sh at SessionStart (brief, and
# listen with asyncRewake), Stop (listen with asyncRewake), SessionEnd and at a permission prompt.
# hookwrap.sh keeps the hook's input and runs this build's `handoff hook <brief|listen|notify>` on it in a
# scratch record beside it, with alerts and sleep holds going to scratch files. None were sent.
# Each line's time is when the hook's input was written, a few milliseconds before handoff started.
# "DRIVER" lines are the pseudo-terminal driver's: it reaps the session's process within about 5 ms
# of its end, as a shell would. "the binding was written" is the time on the binding file as the run ended.
# In each run the driver gives the session implementer (`handoff role implementer`, with the session's
# id and CLAUDE_PID), lets it finish one turn, and then types /background or /fork.
# Session ids are cut to eight characters. The background sessions were stopped and removed
# (`claude stop`, `claude rm`) and the scratch project's Claude Code data deleted after this log was made.
#
# In short: in 4 of the 5 runs with the plain wrapper (R2, R3, R4, R6) the new session's SessionStart hooks
# ran 0.1 to 1.0 s BEFORE the old session's process ended, so its brief said it had no role and no
# listener started. In R2, R3 and R6 the role moved at the new session's first Stop, 52 to 54 s later. In R4
# the new session was idle, had no Stop, and the role had not moved when the run ended 107 s later.
# In R5 the old process ended 23 ms before, and the role moved at the start. In R1 the wrapper's own
# logging delayed handoff by about 0.2 s, which was enough for the role to move at the start.

